Nature provides a pool of catalysts to operate most effectively under physiological conditions and on a narrow range of natural substrates. However naturally occurring enzymes are often not suitable for biocatalytic processes without further tailoring or redesign of the enzyme itself in order to fine-tune substrate specificity and activity. The pioneering work of Frances Arnold and Pim Stemmer on directed evolution set the ground for implementing evolution of enzymes for the synthesis of technological useful compounds [1–3]. The first part of this review describes the combination of laboratory evolution techniques with computational methods leading to more efficient and effective catalysts to generate the desired enhancement of their biocatalytic properties for the preparation of more complex, higher value materials or bulky optically active molecules. Besides that, there is a particular interest in using these natural biocatalysts beyond just creating chiral centres, thus the application of enzymes in multistep processes employing designed recombinant whole-cell expressing multiple enzymes or two or more sequential bio- or chemocatalysed transformations, gained significant attention and will be reviewed in the second part. The final section will discuss the use of in silico enzyme design coupled with directed evolution techniques resulting in the development of biological catalysts with far reaching implications for the possible design of enzyme catalysts in the future.
## Introduction
Over the past few years the use of enzymes as biocatalysts for the introduction of enantiopure active compounds has become an established manufacturing process in the speciality and pharmaceutical industry. The employment of biocatalysts is attractive for synthetic organic chemists as a consequence of the smart insertion of chirality and selectivity with regard to regio- and stereochemistry in molecules and the simple usage in technical production units. Thus many researchers are discovering the advantages in utilising these natural catalysts for producing optically active, higher value molecules. In particular as multiple process steps can be circumvented, the natural catalysts are contributing to more economic industrial processes. However, in reality the range of chemical reactions open to biocatalysis is limited, due to the disadvantageous reaction conditions in an aqueous environment near physiological conditions and enzyme reactivity towards more complex larger chemical compounds. This review describes some of the recent innovative developments in the rapidly growing field of enzymatic catalysis, with particular focus on potential applications in biotransformations.
## Rational protein design
Generally, lipases are of industrial interest due to their high stability in organic media, substrate tolerance, promiscuous behaviour and stereoselectivity. In the last few years, lipases have been employed in the enzyme catalysed synthesis of acrylates, which are used as monomeric starting materials for a wide range of paints, plastics and adhesives. The work from Syren et al. on the transacylation of the industrially relevant compound methyl methacrylate using Candida antarctica lipase B (CalB), also known as Pseudozyme antarctica lipase B, and enzyme variants thereof compared to a cutinase from Humicola insolens and a lipase from Rhizomucor mihei, presented a single CalB enzyme variant V190A with increased transacylation activity displaying an influence of substrate conformation on the reaction rates [4,5]. Liu et al. used molecular modelling and site saturation mutagenesis approaches to create a catalyst that performs acrylation reactions with high activity but low enantioselectivity (Figure 1). CalB, has been applied in the synthesis of hydroxypropylcarbamates 4 from a mixture of primary and secondary alcohol isomers 1, 2 with methyl acrylate 3, which can be applied in the automotive industry for the manufacture of protective top-coatings. Docking studies
Synthesis of hydroxypropylcarbamates 4 with methyl acrylate 3.
of the lipase and the four substrate alcohol isomers were used to identify the $(S)$ -enantiomer of the secondary alcohol 2 to be the slowest reacting enantiomer in the active site. Guided by the molecular model, the binding pocket could be optimised by two-site saturation mutagenesis at positions L278, W104 and S47 to expand the active site pocket together with the binding of the $(S)$ - enantiomer of the secondary alcohol 2. The combination of variants at positions 278 (responsible for increased activity), 104 (responsible for decreased stereoselectivity) and 47 (responsible for creating space in the active site) delivered a transesterification catalyst showing increased conversion. Although wild-type CalB showed only $73\%$ conversion in the acrylation of hydroxypropylcarbamate 4 after $6\mathrm{n}$ reaction time, ${>}95\%$ conversion could be obtained by the L278A and L278A/W104F/S47A enzyme variants over the same time period $[6^{\bullet},7,8]$ .
Enzymic methods for the preparation of enantiomerically pure chiral amines based on kinetic resolution have been developed, but these suffer from a maximal theoretically achievable yield of $50\%$ . The rediscovered class of transaminases present an attractive option as they catalyse the kinetic resolution of racemic amines or the asymmetric synthesis of amines from the corresponding ketone $[9^{\bullet}]$ . Savile et al. have described a process that involves in-silico design and directed evolution to develop an efficient transaminase catalyst for the asymmetric hydrogenation of prositagliptin ketone 5 producing the antidiabetic compound sitagliptin 6 along with the respective sitagliptin phosphate salt 7 (Figure 2). This approach is particularly attractive for the production of enantiopure sitagliptin, partly as a consequence of the replacement of a rhodiumbased chiral catalyst lacking high stereoselectivity and the shortening of the process by one step. An initial screening of various, commercially available transaminases provided no enzyme with promising activity due to absent substrate binding in the native enzyme. The generation of a structural homology model using the $(R)$ -selective ATA-117 transaminase from Arthrobacter sp. revealed steric interference in the small binding pocket and undesired interactions in the large binding pocket. Initial engineering of the enzyme using saturation mutagenesis provided an enzyme variant containing four mutations (three in the small trifluorophenyl binding pocket and one in the large one) possessing low activity towards the desired substrate 5. Over the course of several rounds of directed evolution and a simultaneous optimisation of the reactions parameters, several transaminase variants were identified. The best variant was able to convert $200~\mathrm{g/L}$ prositagliptin ketone 5 to sitagliptin 6 with $599\%$ enantiomeric excess accepting $50\%$ DMSO, high concentrations of iso-propylamine and high reaction temperature $[10^{\bullet\bullet}]$ .
Arylmalonate decarboxylase (AMDase) from Bordetella bronchiseptica catalyses the enantioselective decarboxylation of arylmalonates to carbon dioxide and arylpropionates (Figure 3) in a reaction independent of cofactors or metal ions. These enzymes are reported to catalyse the decarboxylation of $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -arylmalonates [11]. However, no AMDase so far has demonstrated the decarboxylation of malonates, which do not possess $\upalpha$ -aryl substituents. In this light, Okrasa et al. reported a new crystal structure of the AMDase from Bordetella bronchiseptica with benzylphosphonate bound in the active site to help overcoming the limited substrate spectrum of converting only $\upalpha$ - arylmalonates. Initial screening of phenylmalonate derivatives and a range of substrates, possessing alkenyl substituents using AMDase from Bordetella bronchiseptica and Mesorhizobium sp. BNC1 resulted in decarboxylation products with high enantiomeric excesses $99\%$ $(R)$ - enantiomer). On the basis of the novel structure, the large aryl/alkenyl binding pocket could be optimised by saturation mutagenesis of three positions while the small, hydrophobic cavity could be modified by two positions following a strategy similar to iterative saturation mutagenesis. Variants were selected with improved activity and high stereoselectivity $(99\%$ $(R)$ -enantiomer) towards a range of $\upalpha$ -aryl and $\upalpha$ -alkenyl malonates (Figure 3). The best enzyme variant (M159V) performed the decarboxylation of phenylmalonate 9a with 50-fold increased activity. The double mutation P14V/P15G, providing more space and flexibility, enhanced the activity towards phenylmalonate 8a and its hydroxyl-derivative 8b and the corresponding alkenyl derivative 10a. On the contrary, the single variant G190A decreased the size of the aryl-binding pocket thus increasing the activity towards the smallest substrate 10b $[12^{\bullet}]$ .
Bio- and chemoenzymatic cascade reactions In the last few years, ammonia lyases and ammonia mutases have been used in several clinical, industrial and biotechnological applications. The reversal of the ammonia lyases’ natural ability to breakdown amino acids, has gained considerable interest in the production of optically pure $\upalpha$ - and $\upbeta$ -amino acid. Routes to prepare chiral phenylalanines have been employed by an ammonia lyase and amino mutase, which both catalyse the addition of ammonia to achiral cinnamic acids with relatively high concentrations of ammonium ions. The biocatalytic methods to produce enantiopure amino acids are based on the kinetic resolution of racemic amino acids $[13,14^{\bullet},15,16]$ . Wu et al. have developed a novel protocol for the kinetic resolution of rac- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\upbeta}$ -phenylalanines by applying a set of phenylalanine aminomutase (PAM) in combination with a phenylalanine ammonia lyase (PAL, Figure 4). A variant of the PAM enzyme (Q319M) from Taxus chinensis was used for the stereoselective isomerisation of $(R){-}\upbeta$ -phenylalanines 12 from racemic starting mixtures yielding the corresponding (S)- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}^{}$ -amino acids 14 and unreacted $(S)$ - $\upbeta$ -phenylalanines 13. Owing to an unfavourable equilibrium between the $\alpha\cdot$ and $\upbeta$ -phenylalanine leading to
AMDase catalysed decarboxylation of malonate derivatives 8 and 10.
incomplete transformations, the addition of the PAL enzyme from Rhodosporidium toruloides led to an efficient deamination of the (S)- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}^{}$ -phenylalanine enantiomers 14 to cinnamic acids 15 resulting in the resolution of the racemic $\upbeta$ -phenylalanine compounds and furnishing a range of $(S)$ - $\cdot\upbeta$ -phenylalanines 13 in high enantiomeric excess $(>97\%)$ ) with an approximate yield of $50\%$ within 48–96 h reaction time [17].
Epoxide hydrolases comprise a group of enzymes that catalyse the hydrolytic cleavage of epoxide compounds to yield the corresponding diols. The ability to catalyse the ring-opening of epoxides with high enantioselectivity is not restricted to epoxide hydrolases. Halohydrin dehalogenases, HheC from Agrobacterium radiobacter AD1 and HheBGP1 from Mycobacterium sp. GP1 are able to catalyse the selective opening of the epoxide ring in the presence
Resolution of racemic $\upbeta$ -phenylalanines 12 and 13 using phenylalanine aminomutase and phenylalanine ammonia lyase.
of various nucleophiles, such as halide, azide, nitrite and cyanide. However, no alcohols or amines, sulfur-containing compounds, fluoride, hydrogen peroxide, small acids or phosphate nucleophiles are accepted by the halohydrin dehalogenase enzymes $[18,19^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . Szymanski et al. have reported a one-pot four-step synthesis of prochiral $\upalpha$ -halo ketones to enantiopure $\upbeta$ -hydroxtriazoles which are $\upbeta$ - adrenergic receptor blockers (Figure 5). This process is characterised by the use of designer cells (with high overexpression of the required enzymes) containing an alcohol dehydrogenase and a halohydrin dehalogenase with a subsequent ‘click reaction’ [20], a copper catalysed [21] cycloaddition of an alkyne to produce optically pure triazoles. Two designer cell constructs, possessing opposite stereoselectivity, were created: (i) CT cells comprising a $(R)$ -selective alcohol dehydrogenase from Thermoanaerobacter pseudethanolicus (AdhT) and a $(R)$ -selective HheC halohydrin dehalogenase and (ii) BL cells containing an $(S)$ -selective alcohol dehydrogenase from Lactobacillus brevis (AdhL) and $\mathrm{HheB_{GP1}}$ dehalogenase. To fully exploit the potential of this multistep catalytic process, biotransformations were performed combining designer cells CT or BL with the click reaction. First, the applicability of this cascade was tested using CT designer cells and chloroacetophenone derivatives bearing electron-withdrawing groups in the para-position of the phenyl moiety and aliphatic substrates. Thus, the synthesis of the $(R)$ -enantiomers could be obtained with yields of $18\mathrm{-}65\%$ and enantiomeric excesses of $99\%$ . The approach to access the opposite $(S)$ -enantiomer has been demonstrated by this one-pot four-transformation system using the aliphatic $\upalpha$ -halo ketone 1-chlorooctan-2-one 16 and BT designer cells. The $(S)$ -hydroxytriazole product 19 was obtained with $53\%$ yield and with high enantiomeric excess $(97\%)$ via the halo alcohols 17 and azido alcohols 18 as intermediates compared to the synthesis of the $(R)$ -hydroxytriazole product 19 using CT designer cells with $65\%$ yield and $99\%$ enantiomeric excess [22]. The possibility of setting up a cascade of chemo- and enzyme catalysed reactions in the same pot is a very attractive method as shown by the group of Gro¨ger and co-workers, for example, the production of $(S)$ -monastrol, a molecule inhibitor employed in cancer research [23–26].
## In silico enzyme design
Siegel et al. have reported the computational design of an enzyme activity for which no natural enzyme but only catalytic antibodies have been described. The intramolecular Diels–Alder model reaction of 4-carboxybenzyltrans-1,3-butadiene-1-carbamate diene 20 and N,Ndimethylacrylamide dienophile 21 to form the chiral cyclohexene ring product 23 was investigated (Figure 6, $[27^{\bullet\bullet}]_{\cdot}$ ). In the last years, the Rosetta computational design methodology was used to design novel enzymes catalysing bond-breaking reactions, like the Kemp elimination and retro-aldolase reaction [28,29]. This is the first example of a de novo bimolecular bond-forming reaction biocatalyst possessing high substrate specificity and stereoselectivity $(3R,4S$ product in ${>}97\%$ enantiomeric excess and $47\%$ yield). Computational modelling of the catalytic
Multistep cascade of an aliphatic $\propto$ -halo ketone 16 to enantiopure $\upbeta$ -hydroxytriazole products 19 using engineered microbial cells overexpressing alcohol dehydrogenase and halohydrin dehalogenase enzymes.
requirements for a Diels–Alder enzyme biocatalyst was used to identify the $\upbeta$ -propeller scaffold as a protein fold on which the catalytic active site features of the designed enzyme including the two catalytic residues (hydrogen bond donor and acceptor) and the two substrates could be superimposed. The employment of RosettaMatch allowed to evaluate ${10}^{6}$ so-called theozymes from an initial set of ${10}^{19}$ theozymes from a group of 207 stable protein scaffolds, which were then optimised using RosettaDesign towards active site geometry. 50 out of 84 designs were created and experimentally evaluated for Diels–Alder reaction activity against the substrate and two of these designs (DA_20_00 and DA_42_00) displayed measurable activity. Site-specific mutagenesis was used to optimise the activity, giving two new catalysts DA_20_10 containing six mutations and DA_42_04 containing a set of four mutations with a 100-fold and 20-fold improvement, respectively, in catalytic efficiency above the initial hit. A single mutation of the catalytic residue glutamine 195 into a glutamate (Q195E) as a hydrogen acceptor resulted in complete loss of activity (450-fold less activity), exhibiting the sensitivity of the enzyme to the details of the designed active site. This result points to promising applications in enzyme design for other reaction chemistries for which biocatalysts have not yet been identified $[27^{\bullet\bullet}]$ .
## Conclusion
Over the past century an almost exponential growth could be noticed in interest for biocatalysis. The greater availability of biocatalysts combined with new bioengineering tools contributed to the development of new application areas. The ongoing progress and interest in enzymes provide further success in areas of biocatalysis in meeting new synthetic challenges. The next years should see a lot of exiting developments in the area of biotransformations.
Many future investigations will use combinations of engineered and de novo designed enzymes coupled with chemistry to generate novel pathways to get access to new interesting molecules. In addition the generation of more efficient synthetic routes will help to utilise cheaper raw materials and shorter synthetic pathways to prepare a variety of high grade complex molecules applied in the material science and speciality chemistry. A multitude of factors influence the growing interest in biocatalysis which include enzyme promiscuity, robust computational methods combined with directed evolution and screening technologies to improve enzyme properties to meet process prospects, the application of one-pot multistep reactions using multifunctional catalysts and the de novo design and selection of catalytic proteins catalysing any desired chemical reaction. As illustrated in this review, the united power of screening, biotechnology, bioinformatics and chemistry-based reaction mechanism promise for the expansion of biotransformation in both science and industrial applications.
